        <child xsi:type="archimate:Group" id="id-7e3093f5ab0d4a29a5073565ef770480" name="Legend" textAlignment="1" fillColor="#deddda">
          <bounds x="984" y="216" width="265" height="127"/>
          <feature name="gradient" value="0"/>
          <child xsi:type="archimate:Note" id="id-b251c0cf853b4515a83c7771c189f423" font="1|Comfortaa|9.0|0|GTK|1|" textAlignment="1" fillColor="#000000" alpha="0" borderType="2">
            <bounds y="24" width="265" height="97"/>
            <feature name="gradient" value="3"/>
            <content>- ○○○○○: No Added Value
- ●○○○○: Minimal Added Value
- ●●○○○: Moderate Added Value
- ●●●○○: Significant Added Value
- ●●●●○: Transformative Added Value
- ●●●●●: Maximum Added Vlue</content>
          </child>
        </child>
        <documentation>Artificial Intelligence (AI) offers new opportunities to improve organizational capabilities, increase operational efficiency, and enhance customer value. However, the potential benefits of AI differ significantly across business capabilities. A Business Impact Analysis (BIA) helps organizations identify where AI adoption is most likely to create meaningful value and where its contribution may be limited.

This analysis evaluates the potential added value of AI across four capabilities: **Customer Experience Management (CXM)**, **Software Development**, **Service Delivery**, and **Customer Support**. Each capability is assessed based on opportunities for automation, productivity improvements, decision support, and service quality. The expected added value is represented using a five-level qualitative scale.

The assessment provides a foundation for prioritizing AI initiatives and making informed investment decisions. Rather than adopting AI as a goal in itself, the organization focuses on applications that demonstrate clear business value while considering implementation complexity, organizational readiness, and potential risks.</documentation>
      </element>
